On Dec 21, 3:32 pm, Kent Paul Dolan <xanth...@well.com> wrote:
> James wrote:

>  > It's certainly not as portable as they would have you
>  > believe.  I've got to do some more experiments shortly.
>  > Class files generated with 1.6 at work didn't run with 1.5
>  > at home.

> Why would you expect to be able to run class files
> compiled on a more feature rich version of Java to
> run in a version with fewer features? That's not
> what "backwards compatible" means. That "b.c." would
> mean that your 1.5-compiled files would run under
> the 1.6 JRE, but even that is dodgy. Backward
> compatibility is usually something said about source
> code, not compiled code or compiled libraries.

Not really.  Part of the "write once, run anywhere" guarantee
was that the VM would remain stable.  At least some early
proposals for generics insisted on the fact that they didn't
require any changes in the VM, for example.

Apparently, this guarantee has been dropped.  (I can understand
why, but it does knock out one of my arguments for using Java.)

>  > If that's really the case, and they weren't
>  > corrupted somewhere in the transfer, then forget
>  > everything I've said about portability.  But
>  > otherwise, there's still a definite advantage of
>  > not having users compiling the code themselves on
>  > unknown platforms.

> That doesn't guarantee them that they wouldn't have
> to keep current on their JRE version [which is not
> usually a problem; at least on my system, every
> (at least minor point) update is signaled to me and
> installs with no more effort than just me consenting
> for it to happen].

That's the case for Linux and Windows, more or less.  It's
probably acceptable for a client machine which doesn't run
anything critical, but we validate very carefully every upgrade
of our servers, to ensure that our server code still does
exactly what it is supposed to.  (We've recently started using
Linux for some servers, but they have the automatic upgrade
feature turned off.)

